module gunbc.live_deploy.revision

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }

// WHAT THIS MODULE IS NOW: the ancestry RELATION between two revisions, and nothing about deploys.
// gunbc.fleet_main_revision is its consumer: it projects a merge-base observation into
// revision_relation_from_ancestry_facts. It does not consume a probe fold; there is none here.
//
// IT USED TO GATE DEPLOYS AND NO LONGER DOES (operator ruling 2026-09-07). The prose that stood
// here argued for a freshness precondition on automated deploys: main-push runs are not cancelled,
// GitHub orders queued jobs by when they began waiting rather than by ancestry, so an older run
// finishing second would rsync its older tree, converge, green, and silently regress the live
// dashboard. That argument was sound FOR AUTOMATED DEPLOYS and is now moot, because there are none:
// an operator names the candidate, so no race produces a stale run to catch.
//
// AND IT WAS A SECOND AUTHORITY BESIDE A BETTER ONE. gunbc.live_deploy.member_identity decides,
// per member, from OBSERVATION of the host, whether anything differs. This module decided for the
// whole release from ANCESTRY. Two authorities for one question (DESIGN §3), and the coarse one
// won: on 2026-09-07 it refused an operator deploy whose member plan would have converged
// correctly, because the running host had been deployed from a side branch that main was not a
// descendant of. The relation was right and the conclusion drawn from it was wrong.
//
// FOUR CASES ARE STILL DISTINGUISHED, and the reason survives its old consumer: a naive binary
// descendant check cannot tell 'candidate is older' from 'unrelated', and those are different
// facts about the world. SameRevision, DeployedIsAncestor, CandidateIsAncestor, UnrelatedHistories
// and RelationUnverifiable stay separate variants so a consumer decides what each MEANS for it
// rather than inheriting a verdict this module has no standing to give.

// The relation is OBSERVED, not inferred from timestamps or run numbers. `git merge-base
// --is-ancestor A B` answers it in one exit code — 0 for yes, 1 for no, 128 for a bad object — and
// the exit-1 case is a legitimate ANSWER rather than an error, which is precisely what lets
// UnrelatedHistories and RelationUnverifiable stay separate variants. Two probes are needed because
// a single direction cannot distinguish 'candidate is older' from 'unrelated':
// is_ancestor(deployed, candidate) and is_ancestor(candidate, deployed) together give
// (true,true)=same, (true,false)=advance, (false,true)=superseded, (false,false)=unrelated.
// Equality is decided by SHA comparison BEFORE any probe, because it is the common no-op case and
// deserves neither a subprocess nor a chance to misclassify. Timestamps are explicitly NOT an
// input: wall-clock ordering of CI runs is exactly the signal that is already inverted, and commit
// dates are attacker- and rebase-controlled.

type DeployRevisionRelation
  = SameRevision
  | DeployedIsAncestor
  | CandidateIsAncestor
  | UnrelatedHistories
  | DivergedHistories
  | RelationUnverifiable { cause: NonEmptyStr }

// THE DEPLOY-ORDERING VERDICT LAYER WAS DELETED 2026-09-07 (operator ruling). It classified this
// relation into proceed / terminal-no-op / refuse for gunbc.live_deploy, and its superseded arm
// existed so that a SLOWER AUTOMATED RUN could not roll a host back -- "a superseded run is
// indistinguishable from a healthy fleet". There is no automated deploy any more: an operator names
// the candidate, so nothing produces that race, and the gate refused operator deploys that were
// correct. It was also a SECOND AUTHORITY for a question gunbc.live_deploy.member_identity already
// owns and answers better -- that one decides per member from OBSERVATION of the host, this one
// decided for the whole release from ANCESTRY -- and when the two disagreed the coarse one won.
//
// WHAT SURVIVES HERE is the relation itself and revision_relation_from_ancestry_facts, which
// gunbc.fleet_main_revision consumes via a merge-base observation. That consumer is unaffected: it
// asks how two revisions relate, not whether a deploy may proceed.

// THE CLASSIFICATION IS ONE AUTHORITY. The decision takes ANCESTRY FACTS and the merge-base
// evidence shape in gunbc.fleet_main_revision projects into it. A deleted probe-pair projection
// used to sit beside this fold; keeping both would have been the §3 fork of two places computing
// one decision. Reusing the variant names was never enough -- the vocabulary was shared while the
// policy was copied.
fn revision_relation_from_ancestry_facts(
  same: Bool,
  deployed_is_ancestor_of_candidate: Bool,
  candidate_is_ancestor_of_deployed: Bool,
) -> DeployRevisionRelation {
  if same {
    SameRevision
  } else {
    if deployed_is_ancestor_of_candidate {
      if candidate_is_ancestor_of_deployed { SameRevision } else { DeployedIsAncestor }
    } else {
      if candidate_is_ancestor_of_deployed { CandidateIsAncestor } else { DivergedHistories }
    }
  }
}

// DIVERGED IS NOT UNRELATED. The srv1 deploy of 2026-09-04 refused with 'share no ancestry' when the
// running release was a sibling of the candidate with a merge base two commits back: the neither-
// ancestor arm had been folded into the no-common-ancestor arm, so the refusal named a force-push
// that never happened. revision_relation_from_ancestry_facts is reached only once a merge base was
// REPORTED (relation_from_merge_base_oid), so 'neither' there means diverged; a merge-base exit of 1
// reaches UnrelatedHistories on its own path.
